(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).asep=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep.full||[]).push({
  num: 2, part: `Part One: Concept`, title: `What They Actually Need`, weeks: `Weeks 2 to 4`,
  tasks: [`T1`,`T2`,`T11`,`M1`,`X1`,`X3`,`A2`,`O1`],
  cast: [
    [`Odette`,`FRO's safety engineer. Quiet. Asks one question per meeting, and it's usually the right one.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Week two came in with snow. The plows had pushed it up against the old Centennial Lighting Works sign, and by Monday afternoon somebody had drawn a space station in it with a finger.`,
    `Tiago spent that Monday on his knees in the high bay with four rolls of blue tape. When he stood up there was a rectangle on the concrete eight meters long and four point four wide, HEARTH laid out flat, with a red X at one end for the hatch. Inside it he'd taped Wes's sketch from the first week: two crew quarters near the hatch, the galley in the middle, two more quarters past it, and the hygiene station at the far end. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"Smallest apartment in Colorado,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"Four roommates. No balcony."</span>`,
    `Helen Cho-Ramirez came on Wednesday. She was smaller than her voice on the phone, with reading glasses pushed up into gray hair and a carry-on she never set down. She didn't want coffee or a tour. She wanted the tape.`,
    `She stood on the red X for a long time. Then she walked the length of the module slowly, heel to toe, counting under her breath, and stopped in the square that was the galley.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Helen">"Can I tell you why I'm going to be difficult about this?"</span> she said.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"You haven't been difficult."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Helen" data-tone="dry">"Give it a week."</span> She looked down at the tape. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"Nine years ago I was the flight surgeon on console the night the old station's galley caught fire. A food warmer. Three in the morning their time. I had the crew loop in one ear and the cabin numbers in front of me, and for five minutes the only useful thing I could do was write down the time."</span>`,
    `She took out her wallet and unfolded a yellow sticky note that had stopped being sticky a long time ago.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Sticky note, Helen's wallet">0312 alarm<br>0313 masks<br>0315 CDR: smoke in galley<br>0317 all four at node<br>0331 air clear</prop>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Helen" data-tone="quiet">"Nobody was hurt,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"People say that like it's the end of the story. It was five minutes. One of them had earplugs in and his door shut, and he was the last one out, and I watched that clock the whole time he wasn't answering."</span> She folded the note along its old crease and put it back. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"I've carried this around for nine years. I'd like it to be the only one."</span>`,
    `Then she straightened up. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"Now show me where they sleep."</span>`,
    `Rosie had asked her the same thing about the purple rocket on the fridge, and Nadia still didn't have an answer. She walked Helen down the tape.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Colleen's Flight Notes: ask the people who'll live in it`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Needs come from people`,
        body: [`Saturday Colleen asked her to meet at Centennial Airport, where she was preflighting a borrowed single engine airplane in a wind that made Nadia's eyes water. Nadia told her about the tape, and Helen standing in the galley, and the sticky note.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Good,"</span> Colleen said, running her hand along the leading edge of the wing. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Now you have a stakeholder. Most programs only have a customer."</span>`,
          `The stakeholder needs and requirements definition process exists to find out what the people with a stake in the system need from it, in the environment where they'll use it, and to turn those needs into stakeholder requirements. Its inputs come mostly from mission analysis: the problem statement, the list of stakeholders, early life cycle concepts, early measures of success, and source documents like the contract. Then the work starts. You identify stakeholders, you elicit their needs, you build concepts of how the system will be used across its life, you turn needs into requirements, and you check those requirements with the people who gave them to you.`,
          `Elicit is the right word. People rarely hand you their needs. They tell you what they disliked about the last system, or what they think you asked. So you interview, you run workshops, you watch people work, you walk through scenarios, and you build mockups cheap enough to be wrong. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Nobody ever told me what they needed from a display,"</span> Colleen said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"They told me what they hated about the old one. You had to watch them fly."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Stakeholder needs and requirements definition`,`The process that defines what stakeholders need from the system in its intended environment and turns those needs into stakeholder requirements.`],[`Elicitation`,`Drawing needs out of stakeholders through interviews, workshops, observation, scenarios, and prototypes, rather than waiting for them to be handed over.`]],
        exam: `Know the flow. Mission analysis hands this process a problem statement, stakeholders, and early concepts. This process hands system requirements definition a set of stakeholder requirements, and hands validation the criteria it will use.` },
      { h: `Concepts and scenarios`,
        body: [`Colleen climbed in and pulled a laminated card from the side pocket. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Engine fire in flight. Somebody sat in a chair on a quiet afternoon and imagined my worst day, step by step, so I wouldn't have to invent it at three thousand feet."</span>`,
          `That's what a scenario does. The handbook separates two kinds of concept. A concept of operations, or ConOps, is the organization's view: how Halo means to run its station and where HEARTH fits in its plans. It usually starts in mission analysis. An operational concept, or OpsCon, is the user's view of one system: how a crew will actually live, work, and respond in HEARTH. It's built here. Both are life cycle concepts, and so are the concepts for deploying, supporting, and retiring the system.`,
          `An OpsCon is made of scenarios. A day in the life covers the normal day. Off nominal and emergency scenarios cover the bad ones: fire, depressurization, a toxic leak, a sick crew member. Maintenance scenarios cover the person with a wrench. Each scenario names the actors, the steps, the conditions, and the timing. A good set of scenarios turns up needs that nobody would have said out loud.`],
        terms: [[`ConOps`,`Concept of operations. The organization's view of how it intends to operate and where the system fits. Usually starts in mission analysis.`],[`OpsCon`,`Operational concept. The user's view of how one system will be used in its environment, built from scenarios.`],[`Scenario`,`A step by step story of the system in use, with actors, conditions, and timing, covering normal, off nominal, and emergency cases.`]],
        exam: `ConOps is the organization's view. OpsCon is the user's view of a specific system. If a question asks how operators will use the system day to day, the answer is the OpsCon.` },
      { h: `Keeping score`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"How do you know if this flight went well?"</span> Colleen asked, and answered it herself. There are three kinds of measure, and they stack. A measure of effectiveness, an MOE, says how well the system does its mission from the stakeholder's point of view, without naming a solution. All four crew reach safety within the time a scenario allows is an MOE. MOEs show up in early form during mission analysis and get firmed up here.`,
          `A measure of performance, an MOP, describes how the system itself performs, in terms an engineer can measure: how long the hatch takes to open, how much air the cabin fans move. Good MOPs explain the MOEs. A technical performance measure, a TPM, is a critical performance number you track through development against a planned value and a limit, so you see trouble coming before the review. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"The MOE is whether we got there,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"The MOP is how fast this thing cruises. The TPM is the fuel I check every leg, so I know before I'm out of options."</span>`],
        terms: [[`MOE`,`Measure of effectiveness. How well the system meets the mission, in the stakeholder's terms, independent of the solution.`],[`MOP`,`Measure of performance. A measurable characteristic of the system's performance that supports an MOE.`],[`TPM`,`Technical performance measure. A critical measure tracked over development against planned values and limits.`]],
        exam: `Stakeholder view and mission terms means MOE. A measurable system attribute means MOP. Tracked over time against a plan and a threshold means TPM.` },
      { h: `From need to requirement`,
        body: [`A need is written in the stakeholder's words. Helen's is that if there's a fire while they sleep, all four get out or to a safe place before the smoke gets them. A stakeholder requirement turns that into a statement you can check: who, what, under what conditions, how well, with no design hiding inside it, traced back to the need and the scenario it came from.`,
          `This process produces the stakeholder needs and requirements, the OpsCon and other life cycle concepts, the MOEs, validation criteria, and the traceability that ties them together. System requirements definition uses the requirements next, to write what the system itself must do. Architecture uses the scenarios. Validation uses the criteria at the end of the V to show the system meets the need for real people in a real setting. Set that validation intent now, while it's cheap.`],
        terms: [[`Stakeholder requirement`,`A need restated as a clear, checkable, solution independent statement, traced to its source.`],[`Validation criteria`,`How you'll show the finished system meets stakeholder needs in its intended use, often drawn from scenarios and MOEs.`]],
        exam: `The system is validated against needs and stakeholder requirements, and verified against system requirements. Watch for answers that put a design inside a stakeholder requirement.` },
      { h: `Seeing the whole`,
        body: [`Systems thinking starts with a boundary. The system of interest is the thing you're responsible for, here HEARTH. Around it sit the systems it interacts with, like the host station and the ground, the enabling systems that support it through its life, like the test cell and the launch services, and the environment. Drawing the boundary is a decision. Drawing it too tight leaves out the things that make the system work or fail.`,
          `The crew belong in the picture too. In an emergency, the people are part of how the system behaves. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"No airplane has a landing distance,"</span> Colleen said, stepping down off the wing. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"The airplane, the runway, the wind, and me on a bad day have one."</span> Egress time is like that. It belongs to the layout, the smoke, the hatch, and the crew together, and you only find it by looking at them together.`],
        terms: [[`System of interest`,`The system whose life cycle you're responsible for, as distinct from the systems and environment around it.`],[`Enabling system`,`A system that supports the system of interest during its life, such as a test facility or production line, without being part of it in operation.`]],
        exam: `If every part passes its own check and the whole still fails, the answer is about interactions, the boundary, or emergent behavior.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f2s1`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T2`, title:`Walk it with me`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Helen had two days at FRO before her flight home. Amara had booked her into a conference room with a projector and a stack of Halo's old requirements documents.`,`Helen looked at the stack and then out the window at the high bay. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"I can read those on the plane,"</span> she said. What does Nadia do with the two days?`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep the conference room and interview Helen there, one need at a time, from a prepared list.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The interview is orderly and useful. Helen answers every question on the list. The questions nobody thought to write down never come up.`,
          why:`Interviews are a real elicitation technique. On their own they only find what you already knew to ask about. Pair them with methods that let the stakeholder show you.`},
        {t:`Hand Helen the old station's requirements and ask her to mark which ones apply to HEARTH.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Helen marks forty requirements in an hour and looks bored doing it. They're the old station's needs, written for a different module and a government crew.`,
          why:`Reusing old requirements skips elicitation. You get the last system's answers to the last program's questions, and the needs that are new to this module never surface.`},
        {t:`Spend both days on the tape with Helen and safety, walking a day in the life and a fire.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They walk it end to end, then again with the lights in the high bay turned down. Helen keeps stopping to say things she'd never have said in a conference room: the crew sleeps with their heads toward the hatch, they keep shoes clipped by the bunk, nobody can find a mask in the dark. Jun fills four pages.`,
          why:`Elicitation draws needs out of people. Walking real scenarios in a mockup, with the stakeholder and the specialists together, surfaces needs that nobody would have said out loud.`},
        {t:`Send Helen a questionnaire before she flies in, so the two days go to reviewing her answers.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Helen answers it on her phone at the gate. The answers are short and polite, and the two days go to reading them aloud.`,
          why:`Surveys reach a lot of people fast and they're shallow. With your most important stakeholder in the building, use the time for methods that go deeper.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s2`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M1`, title:`Three weeks`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday Amara caught Nadia at the coffee machine. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Helen's great. I love Helen. But I've got a requirements review on the calendar and Halo's crew office is about to own two of your weeks. How long does this take, and what do I get at the end?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Plan it: the activities, owners, Halo's time, outputs, and what done means for the review.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`It fits on one page. Elicitation sessions with dates, Halo's people named, the scenario set, the stakeholder requirements, and the validation criteria, all due the week before the review. Amara adds it to the SEMP and the schedule the same afternoon. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Now Halo's two weeks are on my calendar too."</span>`,
          why:`Project planning covers the technical work, not just the schedule. You named the activities, the people and resources, the outputs, and how you'll know you're done, and tied it all to the review it feeds.`},
        {t:`Tell Amara it takes as long as it takes, since needs work can't be rushed on a crewed module.`, s:0, d:{trust:-5,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Amara writes "TBD" in the schedule and doesn't look happy about it. Two weeks later she cuts a day of Helen's sessions to make room for a design meeting, because nothing told her not to.`,
          why:`Unplanned work loses to planned work every time. Needs definition deserves a plan precisely because it's important, so it can be resourced and protected.`},
        {t:`Add one line to the schedule, stakeholder needs in weeks two through six, owned by Nadia.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`It's on the schedule, which helps. It doesn't say what comes out, who from Halo has to be there, or what done looks like, and Helen's crew office books her for other things.`,
          why:`A schedule line says when. A plan also says what, who, with what resources, and how done is judged.`},
        {t:`Cut the scenario work to fit the review date and finish the crew scenarios after the review.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`The review happens on time with requirements that don't yet reflect what a crew does in an emergency. The scenarios, finished later, change six of them.`,
          why:`Planning fits the work to the schedule deliberately. Cutting the work that produces the requirements, to hold the date of the requirements review, defeats the review.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s3`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T1`, title:`One sentence`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wes wanted the problem statement on one line at the top of every HEARTH document, and he had his own draft.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Deliver a ninety cubic meter habitat module for a crew of four, per the contract. Done. That's the problem."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use Wes's line. It's accurate, it's short, and it matches the contract Halo signed.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Everyone agrees with it, which is the trouble. Three weeks later the team argues about a fire procedure and the problem statement can't settle it.`,
          why:`A problem statement that just restates the contract describes a deliverable. It doesn't say what the deliverable is for, so it can't guide any decision the contract didn't anticipate.`},
        {t:`Write that Halo needs two egress hatches and a safe haven in a ninety cubic meter module.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Wes points out, correctly, that she just put a design in the problem statement. Now nobody can propose a better answer without arguing with the first line of every document.`,
          why:`The problem statement comes before solutions. Naming hatches and a safe haven closes off the solution space that mission analysis is supposed to open up.`},
        {t:`Write it as Halo's problem: four crew living and working 180 days, and surviving fire or a leak.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Wes reads it. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"No volume in it."</span> Nadia says the volume is a constraint, and it goes in the constraints list right under the sentence. He frowns at it and leaves it.`,
          why:`A problem statement describes the need and what success looks like, in the stakeholder's terms, without a solution. Constraints like volume are listed separately so they bound the answer without replacing the question.`},
        {t:`Ask Helen to write the problem statement herself, since she knows what Halo needs.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Helen's version is vivid and personal and mostly about fire. It leaves out living and working for six months, and FRO has to edit it anyway.`,
          why:`The stakeholder's words are essential input. Writing the problem statement is still the engineering team's job, balancing every stakeholder, not just one.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s4`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X1`, title:`Where the box ends`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Jun had a context diagram up on the big screen. HEARTH was a box in the middle. Halo Station was a box off to one side with a power line drawn to it. There was no box for the crew and none for the ground.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jun">"The crew aren't hardware,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"And ground control is Halo's. We don't build either one. Should they even be on here?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Leave them off. FRO builds HEARTH, so the diagram should show only what FRO is building.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`The diagram is clean. When they write the fire scenario, half the steps are things the crew and the ground do, and none of them are on it.`,
          why:`The system of interest is what you're responsible for, but the context is everything it interacts with. Leaving out the people and systems around it hides the interactions where the failures happen.`},
        {t:`Keep HEARTH as the system of interest and add the crew, host and ground around it as actors.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun adds three boxes and eleven lines. The crew box ends up with more lines into HEARTH than the station does. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"Huh,"</span> he says, and doesn't say anything else for a while.`,
          why:`Systems thinking starts with a deliberate boundary. HEARTH stays the thing FRO owns, and everything it depends on or affects goes in the context, so the analysis looks at the whole.`},
        {t:`Redraw the boundary around all of Halo Station, so everything it touches is part of HEARTH.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Amara asks whether FRO is now building a space station. Nobody can say who owns what on the new diagram.`,
          why:`Drawing the boundary too wide claims things you don't control. Keep the system of interest to what you're responsible for, and show the rest as its environment.`},
        {t:`Add the station and ground as boxes and cover the crew in a separate human factors note later.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The station and ground are in. The crew, the one element that moves around inside the module during every emergency, still isn't.`,
          why:`Interfacing systems belong in the context. So do the people. On a crewed system, the crew's behavior is part of how the whole system performs.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s5`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T2`, title:`A day in the life`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By Friday of week two Nadia had a folder of notes from Helen's two days, a stakeholder list that kept growing, and Helen's sticky note photographed on her phone.`,`Wes came by with Halo's old requirements in a binder clip. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"You've got plenty. Start writing shall statements. We'll figure out how the crew lives in it once there's something to live in."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Write the stakeholder requirements now from the notes, and add an operations concept if Halo asks.`, s:0, flag:`skippedconops`, d:{trust:3,conf:-3,health:-6},
          after:`The requirements come fast. They're also a list of things people said, with no picture of how a day or an emergency actually runs, and nothing to show which ones matter most when two of them collide.`,
          why:`Requirements written before the operational concept describe pieces without the whole. The OpsCon is where you find out how the system is used, which is where many requirements come from.`},
        {t:`Build the OpsCon first: a day in the life, a fire, a leak, maintenance, each with steps and times.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`It takes a week. The day in the life alone turns up nine needs that weren't in anybody's notes, including where four people eat at once in a galley built for two.`,
          why:`The operational concept describes how the system will be used, normal and off nominal, through scenarios. Writing it first means the requirements describe a system that works across a whole day, including its worst one.`},
        {t:`Use the old station's operations concept and adjust it for HEARTH's size and crew of four.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`It's a solid starting point and saves a few days. It also assumes a crew that can retreat into twelve other modules, which HEARTH's crew can't.`,
          why:`Reusing a related concept is fine as a start. A different system in a different setting needs its own scenarios, or you inherit the old system's assumptions.`},
        {t:`Ask Halo to write the operations concept, since they'll be the ones operating the module.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Halo sends back their station ConOps, which is about the whole station's business. HEARTH gets two paragraphs.`,
          why:`The customer's ConOps is the organization's view. The OpsCon for this system has to be built with the users by the team defining the needs, not handed off and waited on.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s6`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X3`, title:`Jun's swim lanes`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Jun wanted to put the scenarios in the model. He'd sketched the fire scenario as a SysML activity diagram, with a lane each for the crew, HEARTH, the station and the ground.`,`Wes looked at it over his glasses. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Halo's going to read a document. Write a document."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Write the OpsCon as a document for Halo and let Jun keep his diagrams as a side reference.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Halo gets a readable document. Jun's diagrams drift from it within two weeks, and nobody's sure which one is current.`,
          why:`A document is the right thing to hand a reader. Keeping the model and the document separate means two versions of the truth.`},
        {t:`Model the scenarios with timing and links to the needs, and generate Halo's document from it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Jun puts timing on every step and links each one to the need it serves. The document Halo reads comes out of the model. When Helen changes one step, the change shows up in both places, and the needs it touches light up.`,
          why:`Modeling the OpsCon makes the scenarios traceable, consistent, and analyzable. Timing on the steps means you can run them later instead of arguing about them.`},
        {t:`Model every scenario down to each fan and valve, so the system design falls straight out of it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Jun works three late nights and models a design nobody has chosen yet. Half of it changes in the trade study.`,
          why:`The OpsCon describes how the system is used, not how it's built. Modeling the design before the requirements exist commits to answers too early.`},
        {t:`Skip the model for now, since Halo wants a document and the model can catch up after SRR.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The model catches up in month three, rebuilt from a document that's already out of date.`,
          why:`A model that starts late starts as a copy. Building it with the OpsCon lets it carry the scenarios forward into requirements and architecture.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s7`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T2`, title:`Write it for the people`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The fire scenario was the hard one. Jun had numbered the quarters along the tape. One and two faced each other by the hatch. Three and four faced each other past the galley, the same walk on paper. Four's opening sat half behind the hygiene station's wall, the last bunk in the module. Tiago had started calling it the far quarter, and by Tuesday everyone did. Each quarter got a curtain instead of a door. It was one of the first things Helen asked for, and nobody argued.`,`Jun's first draft started with a galley fire and walked through the fire: detection, alarm, suppression. Helen read it on a video call from Halo and was quiet a moment. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"You've written it for the fire,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"Write it for the people."</span>`,`Odette had been on the call the whole time without a word. <span class="said" data-who="Odette">"Put it in seconds,"</span> she said at the end. <span class="said" data-who="Odette">"People hide in minutes."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Rewrite it from each bunk, lights out, alarm going, with each crew member's steps and times.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They write it four times, once from each quarter. One and two are short. Three and four both have to go through the galley. Four, the far quarter, starts behind a wall and ends at the back of the line, with the fire between the bunk and the hatch. Helen reads all four and says, <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"There. That's the scenario."</span>`,
          why:`Emergency scenarios are about how people and the system respond together. Writing one from each crew position surfaces needs a single fire centered view hides: who's farthest, who passes the hazard, and how long each one takes.`},
        {t:`Keep the fire centered draft and add a step that says the crew evacuates per procedure.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The scenario reads cleanly. The crew's part is one line, and that one line is where every question Helen cares about lives.`,
          why:`"Per procedure" hides the part of the scenario you most need to understand. An emergency scenario that doesn't walk through what people do won't produce the needs that protect them.`},
        {t:`Add the crew's steps for the commander only, since the commander directs the emergency.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The commander's steps are thorough. The commander sleeps in quarter one, next to the hatch, which is the easiest walk in the module.`,
          why:`Following one person is better than following none. In an emergency every crew member has a different path, and the hardest one matters most.`},
        {t:`Use the old station's fire procedure as the scenario, since it worked the night of Helen's fire.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Helen reads it and says, gently, that the old station's crew had somewhere else to go. Its procedure assumes a module you can leave in more than one direction.`,
          why:`A procedure from a different system describes that system. HEARTH's layout is new, so its scenario has to be walked on its own layout.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s8`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X1`, title:`Every part passes`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday of week three Wes went down the scenario line by line in the high bay, defending his layout. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Hatch opens in four seconds. The galley has an extinguisher. Every quarter meets its volume. Aisle's wider than the old station's. Show me the part that fails."</span>`,`He was right. Every part passed. Nadia felt how much she wanted that to be the end of it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Agree with Wes. If each part meets its own requirement, the scenario's covered by the parts.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-3,health:-6},
          after:`The meeting ends early and pleasantly. Nobody asks what happens to the person in quarter four when the galley is the thing on fire.`,
          why:`Parts that pass one at a time don't prove the whole works. The behavior you care about here only exists when layout, hazard and crew act together.`},
        {t:`Have safety run a hazard analysis on the galley, since it's the fire source in the scenario.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Odette's analysis of the galley is thorough. It's an analysis of the galley, though, and the problem isn't in the galley.`,
          why:`Analyzing the hazard source is useful. The finding here sits in the interaction of the source with the layout and the people, so look at all of them at once.`},
        {t:`Walk quarter four's path with Wes: the layout, where the smoke goes, a crew member half awake.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They stand in the far quarter. To reach the hatch, the crew member in it walks toward the galley, through the galley, and past it. Wes looks down the tape and doesn't answer right away. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"No single part did that,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"The layout did."</span>`,
          why:`Systems thinking looks at the whole: the parts, how they interact, and the environment. The far quarter problem comes from layout, smoke, and crew together. No part fails on its own, which is why reviewing parts can't find it.`},
        {t:`Move the galley to the far end now, so nobody ever has to pass it on the way to the hatch.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Wes points out that the far end is where the hygiene station's plumbing has to go. The change starts a week of rework before anyone has written the need down.`,
          why:`The finding is right. Jumping to one fix skips writing the need and comparing options, and this one moves the problem somewhere else.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s9`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T1`, title:`How Halo keeps score`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Amara wanted measures for the requirements review. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Halo's going to ask how we know HEARTH is a success. I'd like to say ninety cubic meters, delivered, on schedule. That's what's in the contract."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use volume, delivery and schedule. They're objective, they're in the contract, and they're easy to show.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The measures are easy to report and say nothing about whether four people can live there or get out of a fire. Helen asks about exactly that at the review.`,
          why:`Contract deliverables measure whether FRO delivered. Measures of effectiveness measure whether the system does Halo's mission, in Halo's terms.`},
        {t:`Use hatch opening time, air flow and alarm volume, since those can be measured on the hardware.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`They're good numbers and they'll be needed. They describe how the hardware performs, not whether the crew is safe, and Helen doesn't recognize them as hers.`,
          why:`Those are measures of performance. They support the mission measures and belong underneath them, not in place of them.`},
        {t:`Use pass rates on FRO's verification tests, since passing every test means the module works.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`It sounds rigorous. It also measures whether FRO met its own requirements, which isn't the same thing as Halo's crew being safe.`,
          why:`Verification results show requirements were met. MOEs come from the stakeholder's mission and are what validation measures against.`},
        {t:`Work out mission measures with Helen: crew safe within the scenario times, sleep and work hours.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They settle on five with Helen. The first one reads like her sticky note: all four crew at a safe location within the time each emergency scenario allows. Amara reads it and stops. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Halo will sign that."</span>`,
          why:`MOEs say how well the system achieves the mission from the stakeholder's view, without naming a solution. They start in mission analysis and get sharpened with the stakeholders, and later they're what validation checks.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s10`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T2`, title:`From her words to a shall`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Helen's need sat at the top of the needs list in her own words: if there's a fire at three in the morning, all four get out or to a safe place before the smoke gets them.`,`Nadia had to turn it into a stakeholder requirement by Friday. Jun had four drafts on the screen.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"HEARTH shall have a second hatch at the far end so every crew member has an exit nearby."`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`It answers the need, maybe. It also picks a design before anybody has compared designs, and the trade study in a few weeks will have to argue with a requirement.`,
          why:`A stakeholder requirement says what's needed, not how. A second hatch is one possible solution, and putting it in the requirement shuts out the others.`},
        {t:`"Crew shall be safe in a fire," traced to Helen's need, with the details left to system requirements.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Nobody disagrees with it. Nobody can test it either, and Tiago asks what safe means in seconds.`,
          why:`A requirement that can't be checked can't be verified or validated. Stakeholder requirements still need conditions and a measure.`},
        {t:`Keep Helen's exact sentence as the requirement, so nothing she meant gets lost in translation.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Helen likes it. It's also a need, not a requirement. It has no number, no conditions beyond the hour, and no way to tell when it's met.`,
          why:`Keeping the stakeholder's words is right, as the need. The requirement is a separate, checkable statement traced to it.`},
        {t:`"Each crew member shall reach a safe location within the scenario time from any bunk, lights out."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun links it to Helen's need, the four fire scenarios and the first MOE. The time stays marked as to be determined, with the rationale noted: the old station's five minutes is the starting point, not the answer.`,
          why:`A stakeholder requirement turns the need into a checkable, solution independent statement with conditions and a measure, traced to where it came from. "To be determined" with a reason is honest. A design hidden in the requirement isn't.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s11`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T11`, title:`How we'll know`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tiago found her at the edge of the tape on Monday of week four, still in his coat. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"So. Helen's going to ask how we prove it. What do we show her?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Build it from the scenarios: timed crew runs on a mockup now and in the module later.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They write validation criteria for each emergency scenario: who runs it, in what conditions, and what counts as success, tied to the MOEs. The first runs can happen on the tape in a month. Tiago circles the last line. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"In the flight module. With crew. Lights out."</span>`,
          why:`Validation shows the system meets the stakeholders' needs in its intended use. Building the criteria from the scenarios and MOEs now, and planning early and late runs, points the whole program at the right question.`},
        {t:`Verify every requirement, since a module that meets all its requirements meets Helen's needs.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Tiago frowns. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"Only if the requirements are perfect."</span> He doesn't have to say they won't be.`,
          why:`Verification proves the requirements were met. Validation proves they were the right requirements. You need both, and one doesn't replace the other.`},
        {t:`Run the scenarios as analysis in Jun's model, timed, since a full crew test is expensive.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The model gives useful early numbers. It also only knows what Jun told it about how fast a half asleep person moves in a dark module.`,
          why:`Analysis is a good early look. Validation needs evidence from the intended users in a realistic environment before you call the need met.`},
        {t:`Leave validation to Halo after delivery, since it's their crew, their station and their call.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`It saves FRO a test campaign and moves every surprise to orbit, where it's Halo's crew who finds them.`,
          why:`Validation intent belongs early, with the developer and the stakeholder together. Finding out on orbit is the most expensive and dangerous way to validate.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s12`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T11`, title:`Who walks it`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`They wanted a first timed walk on the tape before Jun built the depressurization scenario, to check the numbers in the model. Amara offered volunteers. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"I've got six engineers who'd love to leave their desks for an afternoon."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use the six engineers, rested and in good light, since they know the layout best.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`They're fast. They know where everything is because they drew it, and they're wide awake at two in the afternoon. The times are the best HEARTH will ever see.`,
          why:`People who designed the layout aren't representative users, and good light isn't the intended environment. The results flatter the design.`},
        {t:`Use Halo's crew trainers in flight clothes, lights out, starting from the bunks, and time each one.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Helen sends three trainers who've never seen the layout. They start lying down in the taped bunks, lights out, an alarm on a speaker. The slowest one takes a wrong turn at the galley. Jun writes down every time to the second.`,
          why:`Validation evidence should come from representative users in conditions close to the intended use. People who don't know the layout, starting from where they'd really be, give numbers the model can trust.`},
        {t:`Have Helen walk it herself a few times, since she knows exactly what she's looking for.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Helen walks it seriously and her notes are sharp. She's one person, she knows the layout now, and she's never slept in the far quarter.`,
          why:`The stakeholder's eye matters. Validation runs need a sample of representative users, not one expert who already knows the answer.`},
        {t:`Skip the walk and use the old station's measured times, since they come from real crew on orbit.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`The old times are real. They're from a different layout, in microgravity, with crews who'd trained in that module for months.`,
          why:`Data from another system is a useful reference. It doesn't validate this layout. Use it next to your own evidence, not instead of it.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f2s10`, title:`Need, requirement, or measure?`, intro:`Stakeholder needs, stakeholder requirements, MOEs, MOPs and TPMs are easy to blur on the exam. Pick what each HEARTH statement is.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Helen: "If there's a fire in the night, I need all four of them out or somewhere safe before the smoke gets them."`, choices:[`Stakeholder need`,`Stakeholder requirement`,`MOP`,`TPM`], answer:0, why:`It's in the stakeholder's own words and has no measure or conditions. That's a need.`},
      {prompt:`"Each crew member shall reach a safe location within the scenario time from any bunk, lights out."`, choices:[`MOE`,`Stakeholder need`,`Stakeholder requirement`,`TPM`], answer:2, why:`It restates the need as a checkable, solution independent statement with conditions. That's a stakeholder requirement.`},
      {prompt:`Percent of emergency scenario runs in which all four crew reach safety within the allowed time.`, choices:[`MOP`,`MOE`,`TPM`,`Stakeholder need`], answer:1, why:`It measures mission success from Halo's point of view without naming a solution. That's a measure of effectiveness.`},
      {prompt:`Time for the hatch to open fully from the moment a crew member pulls the handle.`, choices:[`MOE`,`Stakeholder need`,`MOP`,`Stakeholder requirement`], answer:2, why:`It's a measurable characteristic of the system's performance that supports the egress MOE. That's a measure of performance.`},
      {prompt:`Predicted module mass, reported every month against the planned value and the launch limit.`, choices:[`TPM`,`MOE`,`Stakeholder requirement`,`Stakeholder need`], answer:0, why:`A critical measure tracked over development against a plan and a limit is a technical performance measure.`},
      {prompt:`The crew want to be able to find a mask in the dark without looking for it.`, choices:[`MOP`,`TPM`,`MOE`,`Stakeholder need`], answer:3, why:`It's what the users need, in their terms, before anyone has written a requirement or picked a design.`},
      {prompt:`Predicted egress time from the far quarter, updated at each design iteration against a planned value and threshold.`, choices:[`Stakeholder need`,`TPM`,`Stakeholder requirement`,`MOE`], answer:1, why:`Tracking a critical performance number over time against a plan and a threshold makes it a TPM.`},
      {prompt:`"Halo needs four crew to live and work in HEARTH for 180 days and survive a fire or a leak."`, choices:[`MOP`,`TPM`,`Stakeholder need`,`System requirement`], answer:2, why:`It's the stakeholder's need stated broadly in mission terms. It isn't yet measurable or allocated to the system.`}
    ]},
  drills: [
    { after:`f2s12`, title:`Bunk to hatch`, setup:[`Jun's depressurization scenario uses Halo's reference leak: the crew has 120 seconds from the alarm to get all four through the hatch and close it behind them. The timed walk on the tape gave these numbers. Every crew member needs 45 seconds to wake, unclip, and leave the bunk. They move down the module at 0.3 meters a second. Quarters one and two are 2.4 meters from the hatch. Quarters three and four are 6.6 meters away. The hatch passes one person at a time, 15 seconds each, and closing it takes 20 seconds after the last person is through.`,`Arrival at the hatch = 45 + distance / speed. People go through in the order they arrive, and nobody can start through until the person ahead is clear.`],
      fields:[{label:`Seconds for a quarter one or two crew member to reach the hatch`, answer:53, tol:0},{label:`Seconds for a quarter three or four crew member to reach the hatch`, answer:67, tol:0},{label:`Seconds when the last crew member is through the hatch`, answer:113, tol:0},{label:`Seconds when the hatch is closed`, answer:133, tol:0},{label:`Seconds over the time the scenario allows`, answer:13, tol:0}],
      solution:[`Quarters one and two: 2.4 / 0.3 = 8 seconds of travel. 45 + 8 = 53 seconds.`,`Quarters three and four: 6.6 / 0.3 = 22 seconds of travel. 45 + 22 = 67 seconds.`,`The two near crew arrive first and go through at 53 to 68 and 68 to 83. The first far crew member arrived at 67 and waits until 83, then goes through until 98. The last one goes through from 98 to 113.`,`The hatch closes 20 seconds later: 113 + 20 = 133 seconds.`,`133 minus 120 = 13 seconds over. Nobody walks slowly and no single step fails. Quarters three and four lose because of the queue at the hatch, which is a property of the whole layout, not of any one part.`] }
  ],
  quiz: [
    {q:`Which output of stakeholder needs and requirements definition describes how users will operate a specific system in its intended environment?`, opts:[`The operational concept (OpsCon)`,`The interface control document`,`The system requirements specification`,`The verification cross-reference matrix`], a:0, why:`The OpsCon is the user's view of how this system will be used, built from scenarios. The organization level ConOps usually starts in mission analysis.`, task:`T2`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Engineers watch crew trainers walk through a typical day in a full scale mockup and record what they actually do. What is this?`, opts:[`Requirements verification by a demonstration`,`Design definition through rapid prototyping`,`Needs elicitation through observation`,`A physical configuration audit of a mockup`], a:2, why:`Watching users work through scenarios is an elicitation technique. It surfaces needs people wouldn't think to state in an interview.`, task:`T2`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Which statement is a stakeholder requirement rather than a design solution?`, opts:[`The module shall have a second hatch at the far end`,`The galley shall be moved to the far end of the module`,`Crew shall reach a safe location within the set time`,`Each crew quarter shall have its own fire extinguisher`], a:2, why:`A stakeholder requirement states what is needed, under what conditions, without picking the design. The others each name a specific solution.`, task:`T2`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Stakeholder requirements are a primary input to which process?`, opts:[`Business or mission analysis`,`System requirements definition`,`Project assessment and control`,`Configuration management`], a:1, why:`System requirements definition turns stakeholder requirements into requirements on the system itself. Mission analysis comes before, not after.`, task:`T2`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`A team's problem statement reads, "Provide a module with two hatches and a safe haven." What's the main weakness?`, opts:[`It names a solution instead of the problem`,`It's too short to put under a baseline`,`It doesn't cite the contract by its number`,`It has no verification method assigned to it`], a:0, why:`A problem statement from business or mission analysis describes the need and success in stakeholder terms. Naming a solution closes off the solution space too early.`, task:`T1`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Which measure describes how well a system accomplishes its mission, from the stakeholder's point of view, without naming a solution?`, opts:[`Technical performance measure`,`Measure of performance`,`Critical design parameter`,`Measure of effectiveness`], a:3, why:`MOEs measure mission success in the stakeholder's terms. MOPs describe system performance, and TPMs track critical measures through development.`, task:`T1`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`A validation plan built from operational scenarios should test the system against what?`, opts:[`The supplier's acceptance test procedures`,`Stakeholder needs in the intended environment`,`The system requirements exactly as written`,`The approved design drawings and their notes`], a:1, why:`Validation confirms the system meets stakeholder needs in its intended use and environment. Checking against written requirements is verification.`, task:`T11`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Who should ideally take part in validating a crew emergency egress path?`, opts:[`The engineers who designed the layout`,`An analyst working from the CAD drawings`,`The supplier's quality inspectors`,`Representative users in real conditions`], a:3, why:`Validation evidence should come from representative users in conditions close to the intended use. Designers already know the answers.`, task:`T11`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`What does a system boundary define?`, opts:[`What's in the system of interest and what's its environment`,`Which parts of the system are already under configuration`,`Which suppliers are responsible for each subsystem's part`,`The physical outer shell dimensions of the delivered item`], a:0, why:`The boundary separates the system of interest from the systems and environment it interacts with. Drawing it is a deliberate decision.`, task:`X1`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`Crew egress time depends on the layout, the hazard, the hatch and the crew together. No single part has an egress time. This is an example of what?`, opts:[`Functional allocation`,`Requirements creep`,`Emergent behavior`,`Configuration drift`], a:2, why:`Emergent behavior belongs to the whole system and comes from how its parts interact, not from any one part.`, task:`X1`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`A test facility supports a module during development but isn't part of the module in operation. Relative to the module, what is it?`, opts:[`A subsystem of the module`,`An enabling system`,`The system of interest`,`A constituent system`], a:1, why:`Enabling systems support the system of interest through its life cycle, such as test, production, or deployment, without being part of it in operation.`, task:`X1`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`What's the main benefit of capturing operational scenarios in a SysML model linked to stakeholder needs?`, opts:[`The model replaces the need for any stakeholder review`,`The scenarios stay traceable, consistent and analyzable`,`The model automatically produces the detailed design`,`The scenarios no longer need timing or any conditions`], a:1, why:`Modeling scenarios ties each step to the needs it serves, keeps every view consistent, and lets the team analyze timing instead of arguing about it.`, task:`X3`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`During needs work, a supplier learns of a customer need the signed agreement doesn't cover. What should the supplier do?`, opts:[`Design to it quietly and absorb the cost`,`Ignore it, since it isn't in the agreement`,`Ask the user to drop the need for now`,`Raise it through the agreement as a change`], a:3, why:`In the supply process, scope and terms are settled through the agreement. Capture the need, then take it to the acquirer formally so cost and schedule are agreed in writing.`, task:`A2`, domain:`Agreement and organizational`},
    {q:`After winning its first crewed program, a company adds crew safety reviews to its standard development process through the process owner. Which process is this?`, opts:[`Life cycle model management`,`Infrastructure management`,`Project assessment and control`,`Knowledge management`], a:0, why:`Life cycle model management defines, maintains and improves the organization's processes and life cycle models, so the next program starts with the change already in place.`, task:`O1`, domain:`Agreement and organizational`},
    {q:`Where should the plan for stakeholder needs work, including activities, owners, stakeholders' time and outputs, be captured?`, opts:[`In the risk register as a watch item`,`In the interface control documents`,`In the project and technical plans`,`In the verification cross reference`], a:2, why:`Project planning produces the plans, including the SEMP, that define the technical work, who does it, the resources it needs, and how it ties to reviews and schedule.`, task:`M1`, domain:`Technical management`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `Jun ran the depressurization scenario on Thursday night of week four, in the high bay, with his laptop on a folding table at the edge of the tape. Halo's reference leak. Alarm at zero. All four through the hatch and the hatch closed by one hundred and twenty seconds. Jun had typed in the times from the tape, to the second.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="HEARTH operational concept, scenario E2 (depressurization), model run 1">Reference leak. Alarm at 0 s. Hatch closed required by 120 s.<br>Q1 at hatch 53 s, through 68 s<br>Q2 at hatch 53 s, through 83 s<br>Q3 at hatch 67 s, through 98 s<br>Q4 at hatch 67 s, through 113 s<br>Hatch closed 133 s. Result: NOT MET (13 s over). Last through: Q4, 113 s, behind Q1, Q2 and Q3.</prop>`,
    `Nobody said anything. Quarter four's bar, with the hatch time stacked on the end of it, ran past the red line on Jun's screen. It was a short way past, which somehow made it worse.`,
    ((s.flags.skippedconops||G.skippedconops)
      ?`She'd written shall statements in week two, before there was an operations concept. Not one of them had a number for the walk from quarter four. The scenario put one there.`
      :`They'd come close to writing shall statements in week two. Not one of them would have had a number for the walk from quarter four. The scenario put one there.`),
    (s.score>=28
      ?`Wes was the one who walked down the tape first. He stood in the far quarter with his hands in his pockets and looked at the red X eight meters away. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Brooks,"</span> he said, not turning around. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Good thing we didn't draw walls."</span>`
      :`Wes looked at the screen, then down the tape, and rubbed his face. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"We'd have found it at PDR,"</span> he said, without much conviction. Nobody answered him.`),
    `Nadia walked to quarter four and lay down on the cold concrete inside the tape, head toward the hatch, the way Helen said the crew slept. From the floor the red X looked a lot farther than eight meters.`,
    `She called Helen from there. It was late in Halo's time zone and Helen picked up anyway.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"We timed it,"</span> Nadia said. <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Depressurization. One thirty three. The scenario allows one twenty."</span>`,
    `There was a pause on the line, long enough that Nadia could hear Helen breathing.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Helen" data-tone="quiet">"Which quarters?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Three and four. Four's the last one out."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Helen">"Okay,"</span> Helen said, and Nadia heard a wallet open on the other end. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"Now we know. Write it down exactly like you just said it to me."</span>`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch02.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Good Requirements`
});
